[38;2;124;124;124mThe question: If M is a differential manifold, is it always possible to find a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcomplete Riemannian metric on M?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is yes. This is a classical result. The standard proof uses Whitney [0m
[38;2;124;124;124membedding theorem to embed M into R^N, then pulls back the Euclidean metric, but[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe pullback metric may not be complete. The standard approach is to use a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mproper embedding or to modify the metric.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about the cleanest proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mApproach 1: Whitney embedding gives a proper embedding into R^{2n+1} (by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mWhitney's theorem, there's a proper embedding). Actually, Whitney's embedding [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtheorem says any smooth n-manifold embeds into R^{2n}. But we need a *proper* [0m
[38;2;124;124;124membedding to ensure the induced metric is complete.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the key fact: Every smooth manifold admits a proper smooth embedding [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minto some R^N. This is a theorem (Whitney proved that there's a proper embedding[0m[38;2;124;124;124minto R^{2n+1}).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf we have a proper embedding f: M → R^N, then pull back the Euclidean metric. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mIs it complete? [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe induced metric g = f*δ. A Riemannian manifold (M, g) is complete iff every [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mCauchy sequence converges, equivalently by Hopf-Rinow, geodesically complete. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor the pullback metric via a proper embedding: proper means preimage of compact[0m[38;2;124;124;124mis compact. Consider a Cauchy sequence in (M, g). The distance d_g(p, q) ≥ |f(p)[0m[38;2;124;124;124m- f(q)| (Euclidean distance in R^N), because the Riemannian distance is the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minfimum of lengths of curves, and the length of a curve under f is at least the [0m[38;2;124;124;124mEuclidean distance between endpoints (actually length of f∘γ ≥ Euclidean [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdistance between endpoints by triangle inequality). So d_g(p,q) ≥ |f(p)-f(q)|.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo if {p_k} is Cauchy in (M,g), then {f(p_k)} is Cauchy in R^N, hence converges [0m[38;2;124;124;124mto some y ∈ R^N. The set {f(p_k)} ∪ {y} is compact. Since f is proper, f^{-1}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(compact) is compact. So {p_k} lies in a compact subset of M, hence has a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconvergent subsequence, and being Cauchy, converges. So (M,g) is complete.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is YES, and the proof relies on the existence of a proper [0m
[38;2;124;124;124membedding into Euclidean space.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow I need to also prove or at least justify that every smooth manifold admits a[0m[38;2;124;124;124mproper embedding into some R^N. This is a known theorem but let me sketch it.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWhitney's embedding theorem: every smooth n-manifold (second countable, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHausdorff) admits a proper embedding into R^{2n+1}. [0m

